speechtext:
Readmission or admission into Canada of persons of Japanese origin is

granted only to those who fall within the following categories:
(a)	Those whose claim to Canadian citizenship is established and who are admissible to Canada as a matter of right under the Immigration Act;
(b)	Those who have established that they possess Canadian domicile and who are admissible as returning residents;
(c)	Those who wish to enter Canada for temporary and approved purposes.
The total number involved is very small. Persons readmissible under the law are required to produce acceptable evidence of their right to readmission. Temporary admissions are restricted to students and delegates to approved conferences whose admission would be of assistance in the democratic re-education of Japan and whose visits abroad are approved by supreme commander allied powers.

Return at January 31, 1949, of number of cases where financial assistance for full-time farming has been approved under the Veterans Land Act: (a) 3,254; (b) 6,125;
(c)	5,741; (d) in the subdistrict of Winnipeg, 1,307. Records under the Veterans Land Act are not maintained according to constituencies.
The answer is complete, with the exception of paragraph (d). We do not maintain a record of constituencies as such.
